WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:06.920
(electronic music with bass)

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.593
- [Narrator] Being a kid in the '90s meant

00:00:08.593 --> 00:00:09.593
spending most of your quarters

00:00:09.593 --> 00:00:13.481
trying to beat your
friends in Street Fighter.

00:00:13.481 --> 00:00:16.514
The gaming community was
filled with memorable players,

00:00:16.514 --> 00:00:19.622
but one kid seemed to eclipse them all,

00:00:19.622 --> 00:00:22.492
the legendary Tomo Ohira.

00:00:22.492 --> 00:00:26.212
- One of the greatest video
game players who ever lived.

00:00:26.212 --> 00:00:29.656
(dramatic music)

00:00:29.656 --> 00:00:33.823
- Tomo Ohira was this towering
figure in Street Fighter II.

00:00:35.942 --> 00:00:36.800
- A guy that legendary,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.467
that's the kinda guy
you wanna aspire to be.

00:00:41.961 --> 00:00:45.492
- [Narrator] So why did
the champion disappear?

00:00:45.492 --> 00:00:48.793
(arcade music)

00:00:48.793 --> 00:00:51.332
In 1991, arcades went
through a Renaissance

00:00:51.332 --> 00:00:54.209
with the release of Street Fighter II.

00:00:54.209 --> 00:00:56.542
The game re-energized
the coin-op business,

00:00:56.542 --> 00:00:58.732
drawing people away
from their home consoles

00:00:58.732 --> 00:01:01.153
and back to the arcades.

00:01:01.153 --> 00:01:02.033
Up until then,

00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:04.693
arcade winners were determined
by the highest score.

00:01:04.693 --> 00:01:06.453
But with Street Fighter II,

00:01:06.453 --> 00:01:10.351
players dueled each other
in one-on-one competitions.

00:01:10.351 --> 00:01:12.422
The fierce matches and cutthroat rivalries

00:01:12.422 --> 00:01:15.244
made the game into a
bonafide sporting event,

00:01:15.244 --> 00:01:18.004
turning champions into legends.

00:01:18.004 --> 00:01:19.852
And a group of all-stars was forming

00:01:19.852 --> 00:01:22.519
in sunbaked Southern California.

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:27.573
(arcade decrescendo)

00:01:27.573 --> 00:01:29.815
(electronic music with bass)
To get this story started,

00:01:29.815 --> 00:01:32.065
we first need to understand
that Street Fighter

00:01:32.065 --> 00:01:34.904
is a lot more than a
bunch of button mashing.

00:01:34.904 --> 00:01:37.615
And who better to explain
the complexities of the game

00:01:37.615 --> 00:01:41.782
than legendary players Jeff
Schaefer and Mike Watson.

00:01:42.851 --> 00:01:45.914
- What Street Fighter is
really, is a chess game.

00:01:45.914 --> 00:01:48.570
They have six different
buttons that you can push,

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.010
and whether you're standing or crouching,

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.330
the buttons are different.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:53.980
And so you have so much diversity

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.480
of different punches and
kicks in a fighting game.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.061
- Each character has
their own specific moves.

00:02:00.061 --> 00:02:02.459
Those specific moves can
be used in combinations,

00:02:02.459 --> 00:02:04.911
so there's so many different variations

00:02:04.911 --> 00:02:07.200
available in the game.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.730
- [Jeff] Based on whatever
character I'm playing,

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:11.101
I know what distance
that character's weak at,

00:02:11.101 --> 00:02:13.600
so it's all about forming your game plan

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.047
around staying at that
distance to win the game.

00:02:16.047 --> 00:02:17.163
- [Game Voice] You win!

00:02:17.163 --> 00:02:18.522
- This is the bread and butter

00:02:18.522 --> 00:02:21.373
of becoming exceptional at any game.

00:02:21.373 --> 00:02:23.380
That's Street Fighter.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.333
- [Narrator] And if you
haven't guessed it yet,

00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:29.099
Mike and Jeff are really
good at Street Fighter II.

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:31.842
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:02:31.842 --> 00:02:33.501
- I became the best
player in Orange County,

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:36.173
and then, eventually, I
got banned from playing,

00:02:36.173 --> 00:02:38.202
because it was a foregone conclusion

00:02:38.202 --> 00:02:40.710
that I was gonna win.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:41.818
- I would beat everybody.

00:02:41.818 --> 00:02:44.133
There was no one I ran
into that I didn't beat.

00:02:44.133 --> 00:02:44.966
You know, I was a little kid,

00:02:44.966 --> 00:02:46.121
just shooting my mouth off all the time,

00:02:46.121 --> 00:02:48.493
but that was part of my strategy actually.

00:02:48.493 --> 00:02:50.493
We always played to win.

00:02:51.469 --> 00:02:52.800
- The whole crowd was thinking like,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.133
"Wow, Jeff's on his own level."

00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:56.192
You know, no one could touch me.

00:02:56.192 --> 00:02:59.272
I was eating everybody for lunch.

00:02:59.272 --> 00:03:00.952
(soft boom)

00:03:00.952 --> 00:03:03.601
And that's when I met Tomo Ohira.

00:03:03.601 --> 00:03:06.332
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:03:06.332 --> 00:03:08.154
He was about thirteen years old,

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:10.413
probably weighed about 90
pounds and was five feet tall,

00:03:10.413 --> 00:03:13.597
and he was just a little kid.

00:03:13.597 --> 00:03:14.430
- He was a smaller guy,

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:15.826
so it wasn't intimidating or anything,

00:03:15.826 --> 00:03:17.677
but man, he played lights out.

00:03:17.677 --> 00:03:18.978
No mistakes.

00:03:18.978 --> 00:03:20.978
His play's just amazing.

00:03:22.137 --> 00:03:24.288
- He's a gaming savant.

00:03:24.288 --> 00:03:27.717
I musta spent over $20 in
quarters to beat him one game,

00:03:27.717 --> 00:03:29.516
and I never beat him one game.

00:03:29.516 --> 00:03:32.115
I can't win one game off the guy.

00:03:32.115 --> 00:03:34.377
He's very much like Michael
Jordan or Tiger Woods.

00:03:34.377 --> 00:03:35.728
They don't need to talk crap.

00:03:35.728 --> 00:03:37.848
They already know they're the best.

00:03:37.848 --> 00:03:39.957
- He was, hands down, the
number one player at the time.

00:03:39.957 --> 00:03:42.728
I was kinda dumbfounded by it.

00:03:42.728 --> 00:03:45.248
It was like Tomo, me, Jeff,

00:03:45.248 --> 00:03:47.837
and then fourth place is way down here.

00:03:47.837 --> 00:03:49.006
- [Narrator] And in 1994,

00:03:49.006 --> 00:03:51.248
the three participated
in a Super Tournament

00:03:51.248 --> 00:03:55.184
to find out who was the
best player of all time.

00:03:55.184 --> 00:03:56.755
- [Mike] There was a big
Super Tournament in Nor Cal

00:03:56.755 --> 00:03:58.807
that all of us went to.

00:03:58.807 --> 00:04:00.968
- It was like the biggest Battle Royale ever

00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:03.437
in the history of Street
Fighter at that time.

00:04:03.437 --> 00:04:04.866
We're finally gonna find out, right?

00:04:04.866 --> 00:04:07.277
Is Southern California
better than this group here?

00:04:07.277 --> 00:04:08.786
It was literally shoulder to shoulder of

00:04:08.786 --> 00:04:11.166
people packed in like sardines.

00:04:11.166 --> 00:04:13.528
- [Narrator] And you can
probably guess what happened.

00:04:13.528 --> 00:04:14.861
Tomo won it all.

00:04:17.577 --> 00:04:20.088
But then something unexpected happened.

00:04:20.088 --> 00:04:22.487
The world's best Street Fighter II player

00:04:22.487 --> 00:04:25.226
completely disappeared.

00:04:25.226 --> 00:04:27.385
(dramatic electronic music)

00:04:27.385 --> 00:04:28.586
- He just quit.

00:04:28.586 --> 00:04:30.195
That was it.

00:04:30.195 --> 00:04:33.808
He never played Street
Fighter again after that.

00:04:33.808 --> 00:04:35.332
- I felt a little empty, I guess.

00:04:35.332 --> 00:04:36.424
He was my competition,

00:04:36.424 --> 00:04:38.195
he was always the one that would beat me.

00:04:38.195 --> 00:04:41.778
He just finally walked
off into the sunset.

00:04:43.137 --> 00:04:45.698
- [Narrator] Questions
haunted the gaming community.

00:04:45.698 --> 00:04:47.106
Where is Tomo?

00:04:47.106 --> 00:04:48.978
Where is "The Champion"?

00:04:48.978 --> 00:04:51.326
- [Jeff] Everybody asked
about him for years.

00:04:51.326 --> 00:04:52.159
- [Mike] His name rung out

00:04:52.159 --> 00:04:53.968
through the arcades in
Southern California.

00:04:53.968 --> 00:04:56.888
Everyone wanted to find
Tomo and play against him.

00:04:56.888 --> 00:04:57.721
- [Narrator] But they never got

00:04:57.721 --> 00:04:59.138
that opportunity.
- [Game Voice] Three!

00:04:59.138 --> 00:04:59.971
Two!

00:04:59.971 --> 00:05:01.384
- [Narrator] The original gaming savant...

00:05:01.384 --> 00:05:02.217
- [Game Voice] One!

00:05:02.217 --> 00:05:04.050
- [Narrator] Was gone.

00:05:05.477 --> 00:05:06.528
So at this point,

00:05:06.528 --> 00:05:08.336
you're probably asking yourself,

00:05:08.336 --> 00:05:11.834
"What became of the legendary Tomo Ohira?"

00:05:11.834 --> 00:05:13.346
- This is my life now.

00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:14.253
Getting balls out of the tree

00:05:14.253 --> 00:05:16.666
for the neighbor's kids.

00:05:16.666 --> 00:05:20.083
(light, xylophone music)

00:05:22.426 --> 00:05:24.386
- My name is Tomo Ohira,

00:05:24.386 --> 00:05:28.184
I used to play Street
Fighter, long, long time ago.

00:05:28.184 --> 00:05:31.205
I was known as the "First
Champion of Street Fighter."

00:05:31.205 --> 00:05:34.373
And the feeling was just incredible.

00:05:34.373 --> 00:05:36.005
(music adds heavy bass)

00:05:36.005 --> 00:05:38.172
I think people were a
little bit surprised by

00:05:38.172 --> 00:05:39.144
me leaving kinda suddenly,

00:05:39.144 --> 00:05:40.933
because I had such a passion for it.

00:05:40.933 --> 00:05:42.133
But eventually, it went from,

00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:44.291
"Yes I won! Oh my God,
I can't believe it!"

00:05:44.291 --> 00:05:47.024
to, "Oh, okay, good, I didn't lose today,

00:05:47.024 --> 00:05:48.832
"so now I don't have to be upset tonight."

00:05:48.832 --> 00:05:51.522
And that's when I fell out of it.

00:05:51.522 --> 00:05:52.962
- When we became adults, right,

00:05:52.962 --> 00:05:54.051
you don't have the eight hours a day

00:05:54.051 --> 00:05:57.482
to put into becoming like
he was when he was a kid.

00:05:57.482 --> 00:05:59.145
- He accomplished what he wanted,

00:05:59.145 --> 00:06:00.914
he left on top and,

00:06:00.914 --> 00:06:02.437
we played one final tournament and,

00:06:02.437 --> 00:06:06.349
he won, so he kinda
walked out on a high note.

00:06:06.349 --> 00:06:07.325
- When something is done,

00:06:07.325 --> 00:06:10.882
I think you should realize
it, face it, and let it go.

00:06:10.882 --> 00:06:13.299
So, now my passion is family.

00:06:14.834 --> 00:06:16.802
I have twin kids that are two years old,

00:06:16.802 --> 00:06:18.762
and as soon as they're old enough,

00:06:18.762 --> 00:06:21.613
I will hand them a gaming pad,

00:06:21.613 --> 00:06:23.080
and see what they can do.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:24.304
Can you say, "I win!"

00:06:24.304 --> 00:06:25.137
- I win!
- I win!

00:06:25.137 --> 00:06:25.970
- Yeah!

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:27.263
Say it louder! "I win!"

00:06:27.263 --> 00:06:28.274
- I win!
- I win!

00:06:28.274 --> 00:06:29.107
- Yeah!

00:06:30.376 --> 00:06:31.560
That's my life now.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:32.826
And if I had to turn the clock back,

00:06:32.826 --> 00:06:33.864
and do it all over again,

00:06:33.864 --> 00:06:36.114
I would not change a thing.

00:06:38.202 --> 00:06:40.535
(bell tone)

